.TH JOBS 1
.SH NAME
jobs, fg, bg \- manage interactive shell jobs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B jobs
.br
.B fg
.RI [ %job ]
.br
.B bg
.RI [ %job ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
These are builtins of the interactive Bourne shell. External commands,
pipelines and background compound commands run in distinct process groups.
Ctrl-C interrupts the foreground group. Ctrl-Z stops it and returns the
terminal to the shell. Jobs lists tracked jobs and their process-group IDs.
Fg hands the terminal back to a job and continues it; bg continues it while
the shell retains the terminal. The default is the highest numbered active
job. Select a job by the printed bracketed number, optionally prefixed by %.
.PP
A background job that reads its controlling terminal stops with SIGTTIN.
Background writes are allowed unless stty tostop is enabled. Terminal-changing
operations also honor foreground ownership. The shell ignores its own
terminal stop signals and restores its terminal settings before each prompt.
Fg restores the stopped job's terminal settings before resuming it.
.PP
Wait PID (or wait without arguments) can wait for background jobs without
handing them the terminal. A pipeline reports its last command's status.
The shell sends SIGHUP and SIGCONT to remaining jobs on exit.
.PP
Job control requires an interactive controlling terminal. Noninteractive
scripts retain their historical process and wait behavior. Shell functions,
modern substitution syntax, and the POSIX set -m interface are not provided.
.SH SEE ALSO
sh(1), kill(1), ps(1), ptysh(1)
